docs/governance.md      # Phoenix Print Farm — Governance & Sovereignty Framework (2026)

This document defines the governance model for the Phoenix Print Farm.
It establishes how authority, control, compliance, and sovereignty are
enforced across autonomous manufacturing systems without relying on
foreign vendors, platforms, or jurisdictions.

Phoenix governance is **structural, not contractual**.

---

## 1. Governance Philosophy

Traditional manufacturing governance relies on:
- Vendor contracts
- External certifications
- Trust-based supply chains

Phoenix replaces this with:
- Software-enforced policy
- Cryptographic authorization
- Local jurisdictional control
- Deterministic auditability

Authority is encoded into the system itself.

---

## 2. Sovereign Control Model

Each Phoenix Print Farm operates under a defined jurisdiction.

Core principles:
- Manufacturing authority remains local
- No external entity can disable production
- No foreign platform controls execution rights
- Policies are enforced at runtime, not by agreement

Sovereignty is **operational**, not symbolic.

---

## 3. Governance Roles

### 3.1 Governing Authority
- Defines jurisdictional manufacturing policy
- Approves export permissions and restrictions
- Sets compliance and audit requirements

This may be a government agency, public authority,
or nationally chartered entity.

### 3.2 System Steward
- Maintains system integrity
- Applies approved policy updates
- Oversees security posture

The steward cannot override governance policy.

### 3.3 Independent Audit Authority
- Reviews immutable logs
- Verifies compliance
- Certifies system behavior, not vendor claims

Auditors observe — they do not operate.

---

## 4. Policy Enforcement Layer

All governance rules are enforced through software.

Examples:
- Which designs may be manufactured
- Where files may be executed
- Which materials are approved
- Whether export is permitted

Policies are:
- Versioned
- Signed
- Non-bypassable at runtime

No manual override exists outside governance authority.

---

## 5. Design & Production Authorization

### 5.1 Design Approval
- Files must be authorized before production
- Authorization includes scope, volume, and location
- Unauthorized files cannot execute

### 5.2 Production Rights
- Rights are bound to jurisdiction
- Time-limited where required
- Revocable through policy updates

Production authority is not transferable by operators.

---

## 6. Audit, Compliance & Transparency

Phoenix enforces compliance by default.

- Every job is logged
- Every part is traceable
- Every policy decision is recorded

Logs are:
- Immutable
- Locally stored
- Exportable for review

Compliance does not depend on trust — it is provable.

---

## 7. Export & Cross-Border Manufacturing

Cross-border manufacturing is opt-in and controlled.

- Explicit authorization required
- Jurisdictional rules enforced at execution
- Full traceability preserved

No default global execution exists.

---

## 8. Independence from External Control

Phoenix governance explicitly excludes:

- Mandatory foreign cloud services
- External DRM platforms
- Vendor kill switches
- Remote disablement mechanisms

System updates:
- Are locally approved
- Are cryptographically verified
- Cannot alter sovereignty guarantees

---

## 9. Failure, Override & Emergency Authority

In extreme scenarios:
- Emergency policies may be activated
- Scope is limited and logged
- Automatic expiration applies

There is no permanent emergency mode.

---

## 10. Governance Continuity

Governance persists even if:
- Network connectivity is lost
- External services fail
- Political or economic pressure is applied

Manufacturing authority remains local,
auditable, and enforceable.

---

## 11. Summary

Phoenix Print Farm governance provides:

- Sovereign manufacturing control
- Software-enforced compliance
- Vendor-independent authority
- Transparent, auditable operations

This framework ensures Phoenix is not merely
a manufacturing platform, but **national industrial infrastructure**.
